/* book: lead: A tray absorber is a column of plates: gas rises, liquid falls, and each plate brings the two streams close to equilibrium, so one plate counts as one theoretical stage. Counting stages on a Y–X plot sizes the column. The absorption factor A = L/(mG) turns that count into one short equation, used to size absorbers for natural-gas sweetening, refinery gas treating and ammonia synthesis gas. */
/* book Figure 3.5.1: Stepping off the worked example between the operating line and Y* = 2X, from the top (stage 1) down. The fourth step passes the entering gas, 0.0526, so four theoretical stages do the job, as the Kremser equation (N = 3.96) says. */
